1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:03,880
Perhaps the most important thing to know
about crime is that it doesn’t happen at

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:09,880
random. Crime is heavily concentrated in several
different ways. We know that a small number of

3
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:15,000
offenders are responsible for a big chunk of
all crime. We know that unfortunately a small

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,240
number of people are at higher risk of being
victims of crime over and over again. And we

5
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:26,080
also know that crime is heavily concentrated in
a small number of places. So the reason we put

6
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:32,600
crime on maps is to help us understand where
different types of crime happen most often.

7
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:38,120
Take this map of homicides in the city of
Medellin in Columbia. We can see straightaway that

8
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:43,520
homicides aren’t evenly distributed throughout
the city – a big proportion of all the homicides

9
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:48,960
happen in a small number of places. There are
more homicides in the city centre and very few

10
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:54,800
in some outlying districts. If we wanted to set
up a project to prevent homicides in Medellin,

11
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:58,760
or we wanted to know where it would be
best to build a new trauma hospital,

12
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:02,320
the information on this map could be very useful.

13
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:07,680
Researchers have done lots of work to find out
exactly how concentrated crime is in particular

14
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:14,320
places. Generally, this has found that about half
of crime happens on about five percent of streets,

15
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:20,440
with violent crimes tending to be slightly more
concentrated than property crimes. That also means

16
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:26,520
that most places experience either very little
crime or no crime at all. This rule – that most

17
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:32,920
crime happens in a small minority of places
– is known as the law of crime concentration.

18
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:36,800
The early research on this topic was
mostly done in the United States,

19
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:42,160
with researchers finding that half of crime in
Seattle happened on five percent of streets,

20
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:47,520
while in Cincinnati half of crime
happened on six percent of streets.

21
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:52,040
More recent research has found similar
results for other countries. For example

22
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:57,240
half of violence in Oslo in Norway
happens in six percent of small areas,

23
00:01:57,240 --> 00:02:03,760
while in Kaduna in Nigeria half of burglaries
happen at about eight percent of addresses.

24
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:07,640
In fact, this heavy concentration of
crime in a small number of places has

25
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:12,520
been found for every type of crime in every
country that researchers have studied.

26
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:16,240
It’s important to note when we’re
talking about crime concentration

27
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:20,520
that crime is primarily concentrated at
small places such as individual addresses

28
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:25,560
or streets. Even in an area that might be
described as a high crime neighbourhood,

29
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:31,200
we will typically find that most crime happens
at a few addresses or on a few streets,

30
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:36,080
with the rest of the neighbourhood having
quite low levels of crime. That is why in

31
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:40,720
this course, we will primarily be looking
at concentrations of crime at micro places,

32
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:46,720
such as addresses and streets, rather than
analysing crime at a neighbourhood level.

33
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:51,360
All this means that if we want to understand
crime, or to try to stop it, then it’s really

34
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:57,560
important to know where crime happens. And
that’s why it’s useful to put crime on maps.

35
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:59,000
To summarise:

36
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,400
Crime doesn’t happen at random – it is
concentrated in several different ways.

37
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:11,000
Most crime happens in a small number of high-crime
places, while most places have no crime at all.

38
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,640
The law of crime concentration applies to

39
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:20,480
different types of crime in
many different countries.